tel 1.0

base graffiti
import soundness

info   web pages composed from archetype traits: each adds a feature, its CSS and its metadata
detail
    _Graffiti_ assembles a web page from reusable parts. A page is a class extending
    `Archetype` which mixes in the features it wants — a `Masthead`, a `Hero` banner,
    `Breadcrumbs`, a `VersoPanel` beside the content, a `Colophon` at the foot, `Keywords` and
    a `Favicon` in the head — and supplies its one obligation, `content`, the central matter.
    Each feature trait contributes its own markup, its own CSS rules and its own `<head>`
    metadata by wrapping what the traits listed before it produced, so mixing them nests them
    in the order they are listed, and independent features never collide. The result is a
    complete HTML document, typed with honeycomb's element vocabulary, which renders to text
    with `html`, streams, and serves directly as an HTTP response.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import htmlDoms.whatwg.*
    import formatting.indentedCssFormatting

    class Welcome extends Archetype, Mainstay, Masthead, Colophon, Headline(t"Welcome"):
      def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Hello, world!")
      override def masthead: Html of (? <: Flow) = Strong("Example Site")
      override def colophon: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Copyright 2026")

    Welcome().html.show
    Welcome().css.show
    ```

    Because the page is a class, the compiler sees its structure: a page without `content`
    does not compile, a slot given an element which does not belong there is a type error,
    and two features which each define a slot of the same name are reported where they are
    combined. Each feature exposes hooks a page overrides directly — a slot (`masthead`,
    `verso`, `hero`), a setting (`versoWidth`, `menuGap`) and a rule set (`mastheadStyles`,
    `menuStyles`) — so a page adjusts a feature without touching its markup or calling
    `super`. Every layout uses only logical CSS properties, so setting `direction` to
    `HDir.Rtl` mirrors the page with the stylesheet unchanged.

    There is one source set, `graffiti.core`, which builds on honeycomb (HTML), cataclysm
    (CSS) and parasite, and compiles for every platform honeycomb does; nothing in it exists
    only on Scala.js. Everything, including the `urlText` given, is exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella. A page needs honeycomb's element constructors in scope
    (`import htmlDoms.whatwg.*`) and, to render its stylesheet, a CSS formatting choice such
    as `formatting.indentedCssFormatting`; the `cataclysm.formatting.indentedCssFormatting`
    import inside the library renders the inline `<style>` element.

refer graffiti.Archetype
refer graffiti.Dashboard
refer honeycomb.Html
refer cataclysm.Css

entry .Archetype   the base of every page: its content, title, direction, stylesheet and the seams features compose through
  detail
      A concrete page extends `Archetype` and the feature traits it wants, and defines
      `#content`. The traits compose through three protected seams: `#frame`, the wrapping
      pipeline, which a layout trait overrides as some decoration of `super.frame`;
      `#styles`, the stylesheet accumulator, overridden as `super.styles + ownRules`; and
      `#head`, the `<head>` accumulator, overridden as a fragment of a new element and
      `super.head`. A page author rarely touches them: each feature exposes its own hooks
      instead.

      The whole page is `#document`, a `Document[Html]` with a doctype, or `html` (honeycomb's
      extension, through `.renderable`): `<html>` with a `<head>` of the `#pageTitle`, the
      stylesheet — inline in a `<style>`, or a `<link>` to `#stylesheetUrl` when one is
      given — and the accumulated metadata, and a `<body>` whose `dir` is `#direction`,
      holding the framed content. The assembly is final, so a page cannot override it and
      silently lose its stylesheet or its direction; `#css` is the stylesheet alone, for
      serving at `#stylesheetUrl`.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The bare page: content, and nothing else
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

            class Guide extends Archetype:
              def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("The guide")

            Guide().html   // <html><head><title></title><style></style></head><body dir="ltr"><p>The guide</p></body></html>
      frame
        label A headline sets the title and puts an <h1> above the content
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

            class Guide extends Archetype, Headline(t"Guide"):
              def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("The guide")

            Guide().html   // <title>Guide</title> … <h1 class="graffiti-headline">Guide</h1><p>The guide</p>
      frame
        label A landmark wraps what came before it
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

            class Guide extends Archetype, Headline(t"Guide"), Mainstay:
              def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("The guide")

            Guide().html   // <main class="graffiti-mainstay"><h1 …>Guide</h1><p>The guide</p></main>
      frame
        label A masthead listed after the landmark sits outside it
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

            class Guide extends Archetype, Headline(t"Guide"), Mainstay, Masthead:
              def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("The guide")
              override def masthead: Html of (? <: Flow) = Strong("Example Site")

            Guide().html   // <header class="graffiti-masthead"><strong>Example Site</strong></header><main …>…</main>
      frame
        label Metadata goes to the head, and a linked stylesheet replaces the inline one
        code
            import soundness.*
            import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

            class Guide extends Archetype, Headline(t"Guide"), Mainstay, Masthead, StandardMetadata(t"A guide"):
              def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("The guide")
              override def masthead: Html of (? <: Flow) = Strong("Example Site")
              override def stylesheetUrl: Optional[Text] = t"/site.css"

            Guide().html   // <head><title>Guide</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/site.css"><meta name="description" …><meta name="viewport" …></head>
            Guide().css    // the stylesheet to serve at /site.css
      ##
      ```

      A page is also a response body in its own right: it declares the media type
      `text/html` (`.media`) and streams its rendered document (`.streamable`), so an HTTP
      handler returns it directly and the `Content-Type` header follows from the value. A
      `Css` value serves the same way, as `text/css`, which is all a linked stylesheet needs.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      def site(): Unit = supervise:
        SocketServer(8080).handle:
          request.target match
            case t"/"         => Http.Response(Http.Ok)(Guide())
            case t"/site.css" => Http.Response(Http.Ok)(Guide().css)
            case _            => Http.Response(Http.NotFound)(t"No such page")
      ```
  refer graffiti.Headline
  refer graffiti.Mainstay
  refer graffiti.Masthead
  refer graffiti.VersoPanel
  refer graffiti.StandardMetadata
  entry .media   every page's media type: `text/html; charset=UTF-8`
      The `Media` given for any `page <: Archetype`, in implicit scope, from which a
      `Servable` is synthesised wherever an HTTP handler returns a page.
  entry .renderable   renders any page as its complete `<html>` element
      The `Renderable in "html"` given for any `page <: Archetype`, in implicit scope: it is
      what honeycomb's `page.html` extension calls, giving the `<html>` element with its
      `<head>` (title, stylesheet, metadata) and `<body dir="…">`. A page which defines its
      own `html` member shadows the extension rather than failing to compile.
  entry .streamable   streams any page's rendered document as text
      The `Streamable by Text` given for any `page <: Archetype`, needing a `Monitor` and a
      `Probate` in scope: the page's `document` is rendered as it is streamed, within the
      supervising scope a server runs under, with nothing buffered. `page.read[Text]` under
      `supervise` is the whole document, doctype first.
  entry #content   the central matter of the page; the one member a concrete page must define
      Flow content: a `P`, a `Div`, a fragment of several. Every feature's `frame` wraps or
      flanks this.
  entry #pageTitle   the document's `<title>`; empty unless overridden or set by `Headline` or `Dashboard`
  entry #direction   the writing direction, `HDir.Ltr` by default
      Set as `dir` on the `<body>`, since `<html>` admits no global attributes here. Every
      layout uses only logical properties (`inline-size`, `inline-start`), so `HDir.Rtl`
      swaps verso and recto in the browser and the emitted CSS is byte-for-byte the same.
  entry #frame   the wrapping pipeline: the content as framed by every feature so far; protected
      `content` at the base. A layout trait overrides it as a decoration of `super.frame`,
      so features nest in linearization order; a page author uses the features' hooks
      instead.
  entry #styles   the stylesheet accumulator: every feature's rules so far; protected
      Empty at the base; each feature overrides it as `super.styles + ownRules`. `css` is
      its final value.
  entry #head   the `<head>` metadata accumulator: every feature's elements so far; protected
      An empty fragment at the base; a metadata trait overrides it as a fragment of its own
      `<meta>` or `<link>` and `super.head`.
  entry #stylesheetUrl   where the stylesheet is served, if the page links it rather than embedding it
      `Unset` by default, so the stylesheet is embedded in a `<style>` element and the page
      is self-contained. When set, the head refers to it with `<link rel="stylesheet">`
      instead, and the application serves `css` at that address, so a site with many pages
      serves one stylesheet.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Linked extends Archetype, VersoPanel:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("body")
        override def stylesheetUrl: Optional[Text] = t"/site.css"

      Linked().html   // <link rel="stylesheet" href="/site.css"> in the head, and no <style>
      ```
  entry #css   the page's whole stylesheet, as structured `Css`; final
      The accumulated rules of every feature, for serving at `stylesheetUrl` or rendering
      with `show` (which needs a CSS formatting choice such as
      `formatting.indentedCssFormatting`). It is final: a page restyles a feature through
      that feature's rule hook, such as `mastheadStyles`, not by overriding this.
  entry #document   the page as a `Document[Html]` with a leading doctype; final
      The form served over HTTP and streamed by `.streamable`: the doctype, then the
      `<html>` element `html` renders.

entry .Headline   sets the document title and prepends a top-level heading
  detail
      `Headline(title)` makes `pageTitle` the title and puts an `<h1 class="graffiti-headline">`
      at the head of the frame, so it appears above whatever the features listed before it
      built. It fills no slot of its own.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Welcome extends Archetype, Headline(t"Welcome"):
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Hello, world!")

      Welcome().html   // <title>Welcome</title> … <h1 class="graffiti-headline">Welcome</h1><p>Hello, world!</p>
      ```
  entry .headlineClass   the CSS class of the heading: `graffiti-headline`
  entry #heading   the rendered heading, an `<h1>`; protected, override to restructure it
  entry #headlineStyles   this feature's rules, a margin below the heading; protected, override to restyle it

entry .TopMenu   a horizontal navigation bar across the top of the page, reusable as `menu`
  detail
      Prepends a `<nav class="graffiti-top-menu">` of the `#menuItems`, laid out as a flex row
      with `#menuGap` between items. The rendered bar is also `#menu`, a helper a page may
      place elsewhere — in a side panel, say — instead of or as well as at the top.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Manual extends Archetype, VersoPanel, TopMenu, Headline(t"Manual"):
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Chapter one")
        override def menuItems: List[Html of (? <: Phrasing)] = List(Strong("Home"), Strong("About"))
        override def verso: Html of (? <: Flow) = menu
      ```
  entry .menuClass   the CSS class of the bar: `graffiti-top-menu`
  entry #menuItems   the bar's links, phrasing elements; empty until overridden
  entry #menuGap   the spacing between items, `1rem` by default
  entry #menu   the rendered `<nav>`; protected, a helper a page may reuse to fill another slot
  entry #menuStyles   this feature's rules, a flex row with `menuGap`; protected, override to restyle it

entry .VersoPanel   a side panel on the inline-start edge, beside the content
  detail
      The verso page of a spread: the left in left-to-right text, the right in right-to-left.
      The content and an `<aside class="graffiti-verso">` of the `#verso` slot are placed in a
      two-column grid whose source order is panel-then-content, so the grid follows the
      writing direction and `direction = HDir.Rtl` mirrors it with no change to the CSS. The
      panel is `#versoWidth` wide with `#versoGap` before the content. It is independent of
      `RectoPanel`: a page may mix in both, in either order.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class WideVerso extends Archetype, VersoPanel:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("body")
        override def verso: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("A table of contents")
        override def versoWidth = 20.0*Rem

      WideVerso().css   // .graffiti-verso { inline-size: 20rem }
      ```
  refer graffiti.RectoPanel
  entry .layoutClass   the CSS class of the grid container: `graffiti-verso-layout`
  entry .versoClass   the CSS class of the panel: `graffiti-verso`
  entry .contentClass   the CSS class of the content column: `graffiti-verso-content`
  entry #verso   the panel's content; empty until overridden
  entry #versoWidth   the panel's inline size, `16rem` by default
  entry #versoGap   the gap between panel and content, `1rem` by default
  entry #versoArrangement   how panel and content are arranged, a grid `<div>`; protected, override to change the container
  entry #versoStyles   this feature's rules, the grid and the panel's width; protected, override to restyle it

entry .RectoPanel   a side panel on the inline-end edge, beside the content
  detail
      The recto page of a spread: the right in left-to-right text, the left in right-to-left.
      The content and an `<aside class="graffiti-recto">` of the `#recto` slot are placed in
      a two-column grid whose source order is content-then-panel, using only logical
      properties, so `direction = HDir.Rtl` mirrors it for free. The panel is `#rectoWidth`
      wide with `#rectoGap` after the content. `FoldableRectoPanel` refines it with a fold.
  refer graffiti.VersoPanel
  refer graffiti.FoldableRectoPanel
  entry .layoutClass   the CSS class of the grid container: `graffiti-recto-layout`
  entry .rectoClass   the CSS class of the panel: `graffiti-recto`
  entry .contentClass   the CSS class of the content column: `graffiti-recto-content`
  entry #recto   the panel's content; empty until overridden
  entry #rectoWidth   the panel's inline size, `16rem` by default
  entry #rectoGap   the gap between content and panel, `1rem` by default
  entry #rectoArrangement   how content and panel are arranged, a grid `<div>`; protected, override to change the container
  entry #rectoStyles   this feature's rules, the grid and the panel's width; protected, override to restyle it

entry .FoldableRectoPanel   a recto panel which can fold away, by CSS alone
  detail
      Adds nothing structural over `RectoPanel`: it layers further rules onto the stylesheet,
      clipping the panel's overflow and bounding its inline size at `#maxRectoWidth` with a
      transition, so a script or a hover rule which lowers that size folds the panel shut. It
      shows how a feature refines another purely through its rules.
  refer graffiti.RectoPanel
  entry #maxRectoWidth   the inline size the panel folds down from, `16rem` by default
  entry #foldedStyles   the fold's rules, over `RectoPanel`'s; protected, override to change the fold

entry .Breadcrumbs   a breadcrumb trail of path segments, before the content
  detail
      `Breadcrumbs(segments*)` prepends a `<nav class="graffiti-breadcrumbs">` holding an
      `<ol>` with one `<li>` per segment, as a flex row.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Guide extends Archetype, Breadcrumbs(t"home", t"docs"), Mainstay:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("The guide")

      Guide().html   // <main><nav class="graffiti-breadcrumbs"><ol><li>home</li><li>docs</li></ol></nav><p>The guide</p></main>
      ```
  entry .crumbsClass   the CSS class of the trail: `graffiti-breadcrumbs`
  entry #breadcrumbs   the rendered trail, a `<nav>`; protected, override to restructure it
  entry #breadcrumbStyles   this feature's rules, a flex row; protected, override to restyle it

entry .Logo   a phrasing-level mark, `logo`, for a page or another feature to place anywhere
  detail
      Contributes no structure of its own: `#logo` is a `<span class="graffiti-logo">` of the
      `#logoText`, rendered bold, which a page drops into a masthead, a menu or a panel.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Branded extends Archetype, Masthead, Logo:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("body")
        override def logoText: Text = t"Example"
        override def masthead: Html of (? <: Flow) = logo
      ```
  entry .logoClass   the CSS class of the mark: `graffiti-logo`
  entry #logoText   the logo's wording; empty until overridden
  entry #logo   the rendered mark, a `<span>`; protected, for placing wherever the page likes
  entry #logoStyles   this feature's rules, bold text; protected, override to restyle it

entry .Masthead   a `<header>` masthead at the top of the page, filled through the `masthead` slot
  detail
      Prepends a `<header class="graffiti-masthead">` of the `#masthead` slot — often a logo,
      a headline and a menu — laid out as a flex row, before whatever the features listed
      earlier built. Listed after `Mainstay`, it sits outside the `<main>` landmark.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Welcome extends Archetype, Mainstay, Masthead:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Hello, world!")
        override def masthead: Html of (? <: Flow) = Strong("Example Site")
      ```
  refer graffiti.Colophon
  refer graffiti.Mainstay
  entry .mastheadClass   the CSS class of the header: `graffiti-masthead`
  entry #masthead   the masthead's content; empty until overridden
  entry #mastheadStyles   this feature's rules, a flex row with a gap; protected, override to restyle it
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Welcome extends Archetype, Masthead:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Hello, world!")

      class Branded extends Welcome:
        override def mastheadStyles: Css =
          super.mastheadStyles + css".graffiti-masthead { padding-block: 1rem }"
      ```

entry .Colophon   a `<footer>` colophon at the foot of the page, filled through the `colophon` slot
  detail
      Appends a `<footer class="graffiti-colophon">` of the `#colophon` slot — a sitemap, a
      copyright line, contact details — after whatever the features listed earlier built,
      with a margin above it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Welcome extends Archetype, Mainstay, Colophon:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Hello, world!")
        override def colophon: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Copyright 2026")
      ```
  refer graffiti.Masthead
  entry .colophonClass   the CSS class of the footer: `graffiti-colophon`
  entry #colophon   the colophon's content; empty until overridden
  entry #colophonStyles   this feature's rules, the margin and padding; protected, override to restyle it

entry .Mainstay   wraps the content so far in a `<main>` landmark
  detail
      Wraps the frame built by the features listed before it in a
      `<main class="graffiti-mainstay">`. List it before the page chrome (`Masthead`,
      `Colophon`) so the landmark encloses the content but not the chrome; listed after
      them, it would enclose the masthead too. It adds no rules.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Guide extends Archetype, Breadcrumbs(t"home", t"docs"), Mainstay, Masthead:
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("The guide")
        override def masthead: Html of (? <: Flow) = Strong("Example Site")

      Guide().html   // <header>…</header><main><nav>…</nav><p>The guide</p></main>
      ```
  entry .mainstayClass   the CSS class of the landmark: `graffiti-mainstay`

entry .Hero   a prominent banner introducing the page, headed by the trait parameter
  detail
      `Hero(headline)` prepends a `<section class="graffiti-hero">` holding an `<h1>` of the
      headline and whatever the `#hero` slot supplies beneath it, centred with generous
      vertical padding.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Landing extends Archetype, Hero(t"Build pages from parts"):
        def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Welcome aboard")
        override def hero: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Compose, don't template")
      ```
  entry .heroClass   the CSS class of the banner: `graffiti-hero`
  entry #hero   further banner material beneath the headline; empty until overridden
  entry #heroBanner   the rendered banner, a `<section>`; protected, override to restructure it
  entry #heroStyles   this feature's rules, padding and centred text; protected, override to restyle it

entry .Description   adds a `<meta name="description">` to the head
    `Description(text)` contributes the description element and nothing to the body.
    `StandardMetadata` supplies the same together with a viewport.

entry .Viewport   adds a responsive `<meta name="viewport">` to the head
  detail
      The directive is `#viewport`, `width=device-width, initial-scale=1` unless overridden.
      `StandardMetadata` extends this trait.
  entry #viewport   the viewport directive, `width=device-width, initial-scale=1` by default

entry .Keywords   adds a `<meta name="keywords">` to the head, from the trait parameters
    `Keywords(words*)` joins the words with `, ` as the element's content.

entry .Author   adds a `<meta name="author">` to the head
    `Author(name)` contributes the author element and nothing to the body.

entry .ThemeColor   adds a `<meta name="theme-color">` to the head
    `ThemeColor(color)` takes the colour as CSS colour text, such as `t"#336699"`.

entry .Favicon   adds a `<link rel="icon">` to the head
    `Favicon(href)` takes the icon's address as `Text` — a path such as `t"/icon.png"` as
    readily as a URL — through the `urlText` given.

entry .Canonical   adds a `<link rel="canonical">` to the head
    `Canonical(href)` takes the canonical address as `Text`, through the `urlText` given.

entry .StandardMetadata   the metadata nearly every page wants: a description and a responsive viewport
    `StandardMetadata(description)` extends `Viewport` and adds a `<meta name="description">`
    from its parameter, so a page mixes in one trait instead of wiring each element. Add
    `Keywords`, `Author`, `Favicon`, `Canonical` and `ThemeColor` alongside it as needed.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

    class Landing extends Archetype, Hero(t"Build pages from parts"),
        StandardMetadata(t"Pages composed from archetypes"), Author(t"Ada"),
        Keywords(t"scala", t"html"), Favicon(t"/icon.png"),
        Canonical(t"https://example.com/"), ThemeColor(t"#336699"):
      override def pageTitle: Text = t"Example"
      def content: Html of (? <: Flow) = P("Welcome aboard")

    Landing().html
    ```

entry .Dashboard   a dashboard view: a brand in the masthead and cards in a responsive grid
  detail
      An archetype composed from `Mainstay` and `Masthead`. It titles the document after
      `#brand`, shows the brand in the masthead, and lays `#cards` out as the content in a
      grid of `<article class="graffiti-card">` tiles, each rendered by `#card`. A concrete
      dashboard supplies only the brand and the cards.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg.*

      class Admin extends Dashboard:
        def brand: Text = t"Admin"

        def cards: List[Dashboard.Card] =
          List(Dashboard.Card(t"Users", P("128 active")), Dashboard.Card(t"Revenue", P("4200")))

      Admin().html
      ```
  refer graffiti.Mainstay
  refer graffiti.Masthead
  entry .gridClass   the CSS class of the grid: `graffiti-dashboard`
  entry .cardClass   the CSS class of a tile: `graffiti-card`
  entry .Card   one tile of a dashboard: a heading and its own flow content
    entry #heading   the tile's heading, rendered as an `<h2>`
    entry #body   the tile's content
  entry #brand   the name shown in the masthead and used as the title; abstract
  entry #cards   the tiles, as `Dashboard.Card`s; abstract
  entry #card   renders one tile as an `<article>`; protected, override to restructure it
  entry #dashboardStyles   this view's rules, the grid and the tiles' borders; protected, override to restyle it

entry .urlText   lets a `Text` be given where honeycomb wants a URL attribute, such as `href`
    The `Text is Attributive to Whatwg.Url` given, at the package's top level and exported
    to the `soundness` umbrella. honeycomb types `href` as a URL attribute and accepts an
    `HttpUrl` or anything abstractable to one, but a favicon or canonical address is
    naturally an arbitrary path such as `/icon.png`, so `Favicon` and `Canonical` take
    `Text` and this given passes it through unchanged. It is in scope with
    `import soundness.*`; outside the umbrella, import it by name.
